<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 1400 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Adon Olam</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="he" dir="rtl">אֲדוֹן עוֹלָם</bdi> / <bdi lang="he-Latn">Adon Olam</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Hebrew hymn about God as the eternal ruler of everything, sung at the end of Sabbath services in synagogues around the world.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>not known (attributed to Solomon ibn Gabirol, Hai Gaon, Abraham ibn Ezra or Yohanan ben Zakkai)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (many tunes; the most common attributed to Eliezer Mordecai Gerovitsch, 1897)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Jewish communities, Ashkenazi and Sephardi</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hebrew</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>not known (perhaps Spain)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>perhaps 13th or 14th century; in the daily and Shabbat liturgy since the 15th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (author not known (medieval; attributed to Solomon ibn Gabirol, Abraham ibn Ezra and others))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adon-olam">Adon Olam</a>, &quot;Eternal Lord&quot; or &quot;Sovereign of the Universe&quot;, is a hymn of the Jewish liturgy. Who wrote it, and when, is uncertain. It is sometimes attributed to the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> ibn Gabirol (1021–1058), but there is no solid evidence for this, and its regular metre does not match his other poems; others have named Hai Gaon (939–1038) or even the Talmudic sage Yohanan ben Zakkai. John Rayner suggested that it was written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the thirteenth or fourteenth century, since it is missing from the prayer book Sefer Abudarham of about 1340, and Reuven Kimelman thinks Abraham ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezra">Ezra</a> the likeliest author, from its style and its first appearance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>. It entered the morning liturgy only in the 15th century and has been a regular part of the daily and Shabbat prayers since.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews">Ashkenazi</a> text has five stanzas in ten lines, which Morris Silverman took to be the original; Sephardi and Mizrahi prayer books add lines, giving 12, 15 or 16. It is strictly metrical, every line of eight syllables. Seligman Baer thought it was first meant to be said before going to sleep, because of its closing thought, and it has been recited at bedtime, on the deathbed and, in some congregations, quietly and without a tune to announce a death in the community. Its grandeur may have brought it into the service itself, where it is sung at the beginning or the end. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sephardic-jews">Sephardim</a> and British synagogues sing it at the close of the Shabbat and festival morning service, many communities at the end of Mussaf, the German rite at the start of daily morning prayers, and Ashkenazim sometimes in place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yigdal">Yigdal</a> at the close of the evening service. When it ends a service the congregation sits while singing it, to show that they are in no hurry to leave the house of prayer.</p><p>For so familiar a hymn there are few traditional tunes, four or five, the oldest apparently a short melody of Spanish origin. The most common is attributed to the Russian cantor Eliezer Mordecai Gerovitsch, who published it in 1897 marked as an &quot;old form&quot;, which suggests he arranged an existing tune. The synagogue composers of the 19th century each wrote several settings, many of them elaborately polyphonic, until congregations turned back to tunes that everyone could sing; Simon W. Waley's (1827–1876) setting for the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Synagogue was taken up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> as well as Reform congregations across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and its colonies. Because of its simple metre the hymn can be sung to almost any tune: congregations use seasonal ones, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maoz-tzur">Ma'oz Tzur</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanukkah">Hanukkah</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> schools and summer camps sing it for fun to &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yankee-doodle">Yankee Doodle</a>&quot; or &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> Farewell&quot;. In 1976 Uzi Hitman wrote a livelier tune for the 8th Hasidic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> Festival, which became the best-known version in Israel outside the synagogue.</p><p>Several English translations keep the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> metre and rhyme, so that they can be sung to the same tunes: a loose one in Prayers, Psalms and Hymns for the Use of Jewish Children (1905), a closer one attributed to Frederick de Sola <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendes">Mendes</a> in The Jewish Encyclopedia (1906) and the Union Hymnal (1914), and two 21st-century versions that draw on these, in the Koren Sacks Siddur (2009) and the machzor Mishkan HaNefesh (2015).</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of praise to God as the eternal sovereign of the universe. It moves from that greatness to personal trust, and closes with the singer placing himself in God's keeping through sleep and waking, which is why it has been said at bedtime and at the deathbed. Its mood is solemn and confident.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Hebrew</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Hebrew</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">אֲדוֹן עוֹלָם אֲשֶׁר מָלַךְ – בְּטֶרֶם כָּל־יְצִיר נִבְרָא,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">adon 'olam asher malakh – beterem kol-yetsir nivra,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lord of the world, who reigned – before any form was created,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">לְעֵת נַעְשָׂה בְחֶפְצוֹ כֹּל – אֲזַי מֶלֶךְ שְׁמוֹ נִקְרָא,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">le'et na'asah vekheftso kol – azai melekh shemo nikra,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>at the time when all was made by his will – then his name was called King,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְאַחֲרֵי כִּכְלוֹת הַכֹּל – לְבַדּוֹ יִמְלוֹךְ נוֹרָא,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">ve'akharei kikhlot hakkol – levaddo yimlokh nora,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and after all has come to an end – he alone will reign, awesome,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְהוּא הָיָה, וְהוּא הוֹוֶה, וְהוּא יִהְיֶה – בְּתִפְאָרָה.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">vehu hayah, vehu hoveh, vehu yihyeh – betif'arah.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and he was, and he is, and he will be – in splendour.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְהוּא אֶחָד, וְאֵין שֵׁנִי לְהַמְשִׁילוֹ וּלְהַחְבִּירָה –</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">vehu ekhad, ve'ein sheni lehamshilo ulehakhbirah –</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And he is one, and there is no second to liken to him or to set beside him –</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">בְּלִי רֵאשִׁית, בְּלִי תַכְלִית, וְלוֹ הָעוֹז וְהַמִּשְׂרָה,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">beli reshit, beli takhlit, velo ha'oz vehammisrah,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>without beginning, without end, and his are the strength and the dominion,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">[בְּלִי עֵרֶךְ, בְּלִי דִמְיוֹן, בְּלִי שִׁנּוּי וּתְמוּרָה,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">[beli 'erekh, beli dimyon, beli shinnui utemurah,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>[without measure, without likeness, without change or exchange,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">בְּלִי חִבּוּר, בְּלִי פֵרוּד, גְּדוֹל כּוֹחַ וּגְבוּרָה.]</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">beli khibbur, beli ferud, gedol koakh ugevurah.]</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>without joining, without parting, great in strength and might.]</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְהוּא אֵלִי וְחַי גּוֹאֲלִי, וְצוּר־חֶבְלִי בְּ[יוֹם]־צָרָה,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">vehu eli vekhai go'ali, vetsur-khevli be[yom]('et)-tsarah,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And he is my God, and my redeemer lives, and the rock of my pangs [?] in the [day](time) of trouble,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְהוּא נִסִּי וּמְנוּסִי – מְנַת כּוֹסִי בְּיוֹם אֶקְרָא,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">vehu nissi umenusi – menat kosi beyom ekra,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and he is my banner and my refuge – the portion of my cup on the day I call,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">[וְהוּא רוֹפֵא, וְהוּא מַרְפֵּא, וְהוּא צוֹפֶה, וְהוּא עֶזְרָה.]</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">[vehu rofe, vehu marpe, vehu tsofeh, vehu 'ezrah.]</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>[and he is healer, and he is healing, and he watches, and he is help.]</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">בְּיָדוֹ אַפְקִיד רוּחִי בְּעֵת אִישַׁן וְאָעִירָה,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">beyado afkid rukhi be'et ishan ve'a'irah,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Into his hand I entrust my spirit, when I sleep and I wake,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְעִם רוּחִי, גְּוִיָּתִי – יְיָ לִי וְלֹא אִירָא.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">ve'im rukhi, geviyyati – Adonai li velo ira.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and with my spirit, my body – the Lord is with me, and I shall not fear.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">[בְּמִקְדָּשׁוֹ תָּגֵל נַפְשִׁי, מְשִׁיחֵנוּ יִשְלַח מְהֵרָה,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">[bemikdasho tagel nafshi, meshikhenu yishlakh meherah,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>[In his sanctuary my soul will rejoice; may he send our anointed one soon,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְאָז נָשִׁיר בְּבֵית קָדְּשִׁי, אָמֵן, אָמֵן! שֵם הַנּוֹרָא:]</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">ve'az nashir beveit kodshi, amen, amen! shem hannora:]</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and then we shall sing in my holy house, Amen, amen! the awesome name:]</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional liturgy. Text from <a href="https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%93%D7%95%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%95%D7%9C%D7%9D" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (SBL general-purpose).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>Lines in square brackets (7-8, 11, 14-15) are the Wikisource edition's marking of lines not sung in every rite; the brackets are kept in en and rom.</li><li>line 1.9: 'be[yom](et)' is the edition's variant notation: 'on the day of trouble', with 'in the time of trouble' as the other reading. Kept as written.</li><li>line 1.9: 'tsur chevli' is disputed: chevel can mean pangs or travail (as rendered, hence [?]) or a lot, portion (the rock of my portion).</li><li>line 1.5: 'ulehachbirah', literally 'to make (it) a companion', with a feminine ending for the rhyme; rendered 'to set beside him'.</li><li>line 1.13: yod-yod (YY) is the liturgical abbreviation for the divine name, read Adonai; romanized as Adonai.</li><li>Romanization: initial aleph unmarked, other aleph and all ayin as '; dagesh forte doubled except in the digraphs sh, ts, kh; maqqef as a hyphen.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
